DAY 2 — SECURITY CONTROLS
Preventive, Detective, Corrective & Compensating Controls | 30–40 Minute Lesson

Today's Goal
Understand the four major control types, know when each is used, and be able to classify real-world security controls during an interview.
•	0–15 min: Learn concepts
•	15–25 min: Examples & scenario
•	25–35 min: Practical exercise
•	35–40 min: Interview questions

What Are Security Controls?
Security controls are safeguards or countermeasures designed to reduce risk by preventing, detecting, responding to, or compensating for security threats.
•	Preventive = Stop it
•	Detective = Find it
•	Corrective = Fix it
•	Compensating = Alternative control when the primary control cannot be implemented

1. Preventive Controls
Preventive controls aim to stop a threat or vulnerability from being exploited before an incident occurs.
•	Access control (IAM and roles)
•	Firewalls and security groups
•	MFA
•	Input validation
•	Secure configuration
•	Network segmentation
•	Least privilege
Interview answer: Preventive controls are designed to stop unauthorized actions or reduce the attack surface before an incident happens.

2. Detective Controls
Detective controls detect or identify a security event, anomaly, or attempted attack.
•	Logging and monitoring
•	SIEM (for example, Splunk/Sentinel)
•	IDS/IPS
•	File Integrity Monitoring (FIM)
•	User behavior analytics
•	Security alerts
Interview answer: Detective controls help identify suspicious activity or incidents early so that the organization can respond quickly.

3. Corrective Controls
Corrective controls respond to and fix an issue after it has occurred, contain damage, and restore normal operation.
•	Patch management
•	Incident response
•	Malware removal
•	Restore from backup
•	Account lockout/reset
•	Credential revocation
Interview answer: Corrective controls reduce the impact of an incident and help restore the system to a secure operating state.

4. Compensating Controls
Compensating controls provide an alternative way to reduce risk when the primary control cannot be implemented.
•	Manual review/approval
•	Additional monitoring
•	Stronger controls in another area
•	Extra logging
•	Policy and process controls
•	Network restrictions
Important: A compensating control is an alternative measure used because the preferred control is not feasible or cannot yet be implemented.

Examples of Controls in Action
Risk / Threat	Preventive	Detective	Corrective	Compensating
Unauthorized cloud access	MFA, least privilege, IAM roles	CloudTrail, GuardDuty	Revoke keys, reset password, lock account	Manual access review, extra monitoring
Malware on endpoint	Anti-malware, allowlisting	Endpoint logs, SIEM alerts	Remove malware, patch, restore files	Network segmentation, restricted access
Network data leakage	TLS, DLP, access control	Network monitoring, DLP alerts	Block connection, rotate keys, notify users	Mask data, stronger logging
Unpatched vulnerability	Patch policy, vulnerability scanning	Scanner alerts, dashboard	Apply patch, verify fix	WAF rules, temporary restrictions

Product Security Scenario — HVAC System
Architecture: Mobile App → Cloud API → IoT HVAC Device → Physical HVAC System
Preventive: Certificate/mTLS authentication, firewall rules, role-based access.
Detective: Connection logging, API anomaly monitoring, alerts for unusual device behavior.
Corrective: Isolate a compromised device, push a patch, revoke its certificate, restore known-good configuration.
Compensating: If firmware signing cannot yet be implemented, use strong network segmentation, extra monitoring, and manual firmware approval.

